[["wrb.fr",null,"[null,[\"c_c68e14808ce1b831\",\"r_88df9c5df0cb8a2c\"],null,null,[[\"rc_2337a6645dabefd2\",[\"Here is what is going on in the `gemini-webapi` codebase regarding that 20-second watchdog and why `luuquangvu`'s comment contradicts what you are seeing.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 1. Where Does 20 Seconds Come From?\\n\\nIn the upstream repository (`HanaokaYuzu/Gemini-API` / `gemini-webapi`), there are actually two distinct timeout concepts that frequently get conflated:\\n\\n1. **The HTTP/Session-level timeout:** This is typically set generously (e.g., 120s–450s) on the underlying `httpx` or `curl_cffi` client. This is what `luuquangvu` is referencing when he says:\\n   \\u003e *\\\"the default watchdog timeout is 120 seconds, while the default request timeout is 450 seconds.\\\"*\\n2. **The In-Flight Chunk/Stall Watchdog:** When consuming the server-sent events (SSE) or streaming chunks in `_generate()`, the stream reader implements an active watchdog to detect stalled or half-closed TCP sockets. \\n   - In several revisions and streaming implementations of the client, the idle chunk loop enforces an internal stall interval—often hardcoded or defaulting to **15–20 seconds** (or calculating\"],null,null,null,null,null,null,[1],\"en\",null,null,[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[],null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,[]]],[\"New York, USA\",\"SWML_DESCRIPTION_FROM_YOUR_INTERNET_ADDRESS\",false,null,\"//www.google.com/maps/vt/data\\u003dAvIzW62PGhAfUQRpZ9e4q2af_7_WOsFiVpXkQpd9LX0zB0dpJdp9NdkGa-o9tBoMCvd_Di6NgmlfyQXVg4MjqFFJU5C9KoCTOCJ6dgPFDBPpFVbqYZh0Zc64U3skCrf4jwF8niK7flGYwqgOmji5U7TBRwp7BHZh3ps424DbYR5MR0cpmx2J9OED3SQgECsR\"],null,null,\"US\",null,null,null,null,null,false,null,null,null,null,\"en\",null,null,null,true,[null,[false,false,true],[false,true,false,false,true,false],false,[[[\"Academic - Other\",2.6100187E-4],[\"Coding\",0.006806461],[\"Creative - Other\",7.8314956E-4